---
/**
 * website/src/pages/privacy.astro — the privacy statement (website plan, Task W1.12;
 * reconciliation findings 16 and 18; decisions D93, D106 and W3.2's privacy defaults;
 * the software part is the owner's request of 2026-10-19).
 *
 * The page a reader is owed once the site measures anything: what is collected, by what, when
 * it loads, how the answer is given and how it is taken back. It is also the page where the
 * choice is always available again — `Base.astro` is asked for `alwaysOffer` here, so the
 * banner appears on this page even for a visitor who already decided, which is what makes
 * consent withdrawable from the one page that says how (W1.12 step 3).
 *
 * **Two parts, because two audiences read it.** The App Store and Play listings need one
 * privacy policy URL, and that URL has to cover the software as well as the site, so the page
 * carries "The website" and "The software" as its two parts. Part one is unchanged in
 * substance: every sentence about measurement is a fact the build holds and nothing is
 * invented. The page reads `site.yaml` through the content pipeline, exactly as the layout
 * does, and branches on `analytics.enabled`: with the flag off it says the site collects
 * nothing, and it names no vendor, because there is no vendor to name. With the flag on it
 * states what the site's own gate does — Google Analytics 4, one page view per page and, on the
 * four campaign pages, the three key events G2.4 names; no user id, Google Signals and ads
 * personalisation off, GA4's own IP anonymisation — and it does not restate Google's policy for
 * Google. Four of its claims are held to the
 * artifact by `website/test/consent.mjs`: that no page carries a form or a field; that the
 * tool it names is the tool the build would load (the page names Google Analytics, and the
 * origin the gate loads from must be Google's); that the withdrawal it describes deletes the
 * cookies it says it does (the script touches `document.cookie`, and the sentence and the
 * code are held to each other); and that the defaults it states are the ones the gate's
 * `config` call makes, in both directions — an option the script sets is a sentence the page
 * owes the reader, and an option the script stops setting is a sentence the page may not keep
 * ("the privacy defaults the page states are the ones the site's script makes"). The last
 * claim, that the page names nothing it cannot hold, is the one no test makes: it is a
 * sentence to read rather than a string to match.
 *
 * **Part two states only what the code and the documents hold**, and
 * `website/test/privacy.mjs` holds each sentence to its source: the CLI's and the console's
 * reach is read from the hosts their sources name, the update check from
 * `internal/updatecheck` and `internal/cli/updatewarn.go` (one GET to GitHub's public
 * "latest release" endpoint, at most once a day, cached on the machine, and the two switches
 * that turn it off), the phone app's two credentials and its secure storage from
 * `mobile/src/accounts/`, and the app's reporting from the other direction — the page names
 * Firebase Crashlytics and Firebase Analytics, so the same gate refuses that naming unless
 * the repository carries the plan for it (`docs/plans/2026-10-19-mobile-telemetry.md`) or the
 * app's own dependency list already carries the two packages, and it refuses the app's
 * carrying them without the page naming them. The app's "no advertising identifier" is read
 * off the tree, not asserted: the scan refuses the platform's advertising-identifier APIs
 * anywhere under `mobile/`. The project the app signs in to is the reader's own — the CLI and
 * the console reach nothing the Fugaro project operates, and the app's reports are the one
 * flow that does, which is why the page states them and says that the app versions carrying
 * them have no switch to turn them off (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-19). The clause that
 * says which of the other things a reader may hold these reports do not touch names the two
 * other tools and not the website: the website's own measurement is part one's subject, it is
 * Google Analytics under a consent that part states, and a software-part sentence calling the
 * website unaffected would be this page contradicting its own first half.
 *
 * **The app's paragraph describes the app as it will be, and says so.** Crashlytics and
 * Analytics are the owner's ruling of 2026-10-19 and are not in the app yet, so the
 * sentences above them are read off the design that will build them, and the page says which
 * version carries the reports rather than implying the build a reader may hold does. When the
 * telemetry plan lands in this repository, the gate's second direction becomes the plan file
 * rather than the page's own "earlier builds" sentence, with no change to this page.
 *
 * **It is measured like every other page**, and D188's ceiling is no longer 250 words: the
 * privacy statement is the one page that is not the site's pitch — it carries what a reader
 * and a store reviewer both need, and the ceiling is re-derived from its own count, in
 * `website/README.md`, the way D192 re-derives the byte budgets. The sentence about the
 * host's request logs stays where W3.2 put it, now as a line of "What this site does not do"
 * rather than a section of its own.
 *
 * **The page wears the bold look, out of the site's own shapes.** The owner approved the
 * redesign on 2026-10-20 (`website/design-ref/`), and the look is written once in
 * `src/styles/base.css`'s section 2c: the glow and the grid a page opens on, the display
 * scale, the gradient text and the card. This page renders those and invents nothing of its
 * own — the headline is `display-xl` with its word in the gradient, the two part headings are
 * `display-lg`, every subsection under them is a `.card`, and the hero's glow is the layout's
 * own `.hero-glow`, which is why this page adds no shape to the shared layer. **No word of the
 * page moved**: a redesign of a statement is a layout and not a rewrite, so every sentence
 * here is still the one `website/test/privacy.mjs` and `website/test/consent.mjs` hold to the
 * repository, and the page's own count — which is what `website/README.md` derives the words
 * ceiling from — is where it was. `website/test/privacy.mjs` holds the look itself: the
 * headline is on the display scale and in the gradient, the two part headings are too, and no
 * `h3` of the page is left outside a card.
 *
 * The page carries no script of its own and no copy of the consent key: the banner is the
 * layout's, rendered here because this page asks for it. The styles are scoped, token-only
 * (no colour literal, so the palette stays the one in force) and add no behaviour.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'

// The facts, through the pipeline the layout uses: a site.yaml this page cannot read stops
// the build rather than shipping a statement about a configuration nobody checked (W1.3).
const site = loadSite()
const analyticsEnabled = site.facts.analytics.enabled

// The repository, from the same file every other GitHub link on the site is built from
// (W1.7): the issue tracker and the reporting route are links a reader acts on, so they are
// built rather than typed.
const repo = site.facts.repository
---

<Base title="Privacy" alwaysOffer>
  <article class="privacy">
    {/* The hero: the page's headline and its one line of lead, over the glow and the grid the
        bold look draws at the top of a page (`base.css`'s `.hero-glow`, which is decoration
        and carries no text of its own). */}
    <div class="privacy-hero">
      <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <h1 class="privacy-title display-xl"><span class="gradient-text">Privacy</span></h1>

      <p class="privacy-lead">
        What this website does with a visitor's browser, and what the software does with your
        data.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The page's two parts. Both part headings are children of this one box and of nothing
        else: the gate reads the page's `h2`s and requires the two of them, in this order, at
        the same level, so a part is a heading and the cards under it rather than a container
        of its own. */}
    <div class="privacy-body">
      <h2 class="privacy-part display-lg">The website</h2>

      {analyticsEnabled ? (
        <>
          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>What this site collects</h3>
            <p>
              This site counts visits with <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong>, to see which pages
              are read. Google's script reports the page's path, without any query, and sets{' '}
              <strong>identifiers</strong> in your browser.
            </p>
            <p>
              The site asks for one page view per page and no user id, with Google Signals and ads
              personalisation off. The four campaign pages add three events, under their own
              names: <code>demo_booked</code> (a booking in Calendly's widget),{' '}
              <code>install_click</code> (a press of <strong>Install free</strong>) and{' '}
              <code>whatsapp_click</code> (a press of the chat link). Each carries nothing but its
              own name.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>Consent</h3>
            <p>
              Google's script loads only after you accept; until then this page has asked Google
              nothing, and the campaign pages have asked their trackers and Calendly nothing.{' '}
              <strong>Decline</strong> loads nothing, now or on any later page, and takes the
              campaign answer back as well. Dismissing the banner — Escape, or reading on — is a
              refusal, and the answer is kept in this browser under{' '}
              <code>fugaro:analytics-consent</code> in <code>localStorage</code>.
            </p>
            <p>
              To withdraw, decline in the banner here: it always offers the choice again, and
              declining stops reporting at once, on this page too, and deletes Google's cookies
              (<code>_ga</code> and the rest) from this browser, with the campaign trackers'
              cookies listed below where they were set.
            </p>
            <p>
              Accepting here is narrower: it answers the site's question alone and leaves the
              campaign question where it stands, since a yes to the campaign trackers is given on
              a campaign page and never by implication.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>The campaign pages</h3>
            <p>
              Four pages — <code>/save</code>, <code>/control</code>, <code>/scale</code> and{' '}
              <code>/crew</code> — are where the ads land, and their banner asks a second
              question: whether the marketing trackers that run there,{' '}
              <strong>Meta</strong>'s pixel, <strong>LinkedIn</strong>'s insight tag and{' '}
              <strong>Calendly</strong>'s booking widget may load. The answer is kept under{' '}
              <code>fugaro:marketing-consent</code>, apart from the analytics answer, and until
              you accept it none of them is asked anything, on any page.
            </p>
            <p>
              With that consent, the trackers run their own scripts on the campaign page, and this
              site hands them the page's address built from the origin and the path, with no
              query string; a script running there can read the address you arrived with,
              campaign parameters included, though nothing this site sends carries them. They set
              identifiers of their own in this browser, and Calendly's widget is drawn on the
              page, with Calendly's own cookies.
            </p>
            <p>
              Declining stops them on the next page and deletes their cookies from this browser:{' '}
              <code>_fbp</code>, <code>_fbc</code>, <code>li_sugr</code>,{' '}
              <code>AnalyticsSyncHistory</code>, <code>UserMatchHistory</code>,{' '}
              <code>bcookie</code>, <code>lidc</code> and <code>li_gc</code>. A campaign page's
              banner is gone once it has been answered, so the privacy page is where the answer is
              taken back. A script already running keeps running until you reload, and a cookie a
              vendor set on its own domain is the vendor's to remove.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>What this site does not do</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>It sets no cookie of its own, and has no account, form or comment box.</li>
              <li>
                It loads nothing from another origin except Google's script — and, on the four
                campaign pages above, the trackers and the widget the second question covers — and
                only after the consent that governs each.
              </li>
              <li>
                It keeps no log of its own. The host's request logs are the host's, and this
                repository does not control them.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </>
      ) : (
        <>
          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <p>
              <strong>This site collects nothing.</strong> It is a set of static pages built from
              this repository's files: no account, no form, no comment box, and no code of the
              site's own running on a server. Nothing counts your visit, and nothing about it is
              sent anywhere. Should measurement ever be switched on, this page changes at the same
              time, and the site asks you first.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>Analytics</h3>
            <p>
              This site has no analytics at all: nothing counts your visit, and no analytics
              script, cookie or identifier is involved in the page you are reading. There is no
              consent to give here, and nothing to withdraw.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card privacy-card">
            <h3>What this site does not do</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>It sets no cookie, and it loads nothing that could set one.</li>
              <li>
                It loads nothing from another origin — no font, no script, no image, no embedded
                player.
              </li>
              <li>It sells nothing, because it collects nothing to sell.</li>
              <li>
                It keeps no log of its own. The host's request logs are the host's, and this
                repository does not control them.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </>
      )}

      <h2 class="privacy-part display-lg">The software</h2>

      <div class="card privacy-card">
        <h3>Where it runs</h3>
        <p>
          The command line tool and the desktop console run on your own machine. They talk to
          your own Google Cloud project, your own git provider and the model provider you
          configured, and to nothing the Fugaro project operates. The command line tool reports
          nothing. The console reports nothing by default either — no telemetry, no usage
          statistics, no identifier — with one exception it asks you about: <strong>Send Crash
          Reports</strong>, a switch in its own menu, off until you tick it. With it ticked, the
          next launch of the console sends a crash to Sentry, hosted in the European Union. A
          report holds the exception's type and the code frames it was thrown from, as an
          app-relative file with its line and column, and no message, no path, no user name, no
          environment and no host name. Untick the switch and the next launch stops sending.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card privacy-card">
        <h3>The update check</h3>
        <p>
          The one thing the tool asks outside your own accounts is whether a newer release
          exists. It asks GitHub, at most once a day: one request to this repository's public
          release list, carrying nothing about you, with the answer kept on your machine.
          <code>FUGARO_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> or <code>update_check: false</code> turns it off;
          <code>fugaro doctor</code>, which you run yourself, still reports your version.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card privacy-card">
        <h3>The phone app</h3>
        <p>
          The phone app is built to sign in to your own Firebase project — the project id you type
          in for an installation — and to read only what that project's rules grant it. Signing in,
          it names the paired device to that project, and nothing else of yours goes with it. Its
          writes go into the mailbox your own installation reads. Its two credentials, a Firebase
          ID token and the pairing key, live in the device's secure storage, the Keychain or the
          Keystore, and nowhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Crash reports and anonymous usage.</strong> The app version that carries these
          reports sends two things to a Firebase project the Fugaro project operates, and not to
          your own: crashes, through <strong>Firebase Crashlytics</strong>, and anonymous usage,
          through <strong>Firebase Analytics</strong>. A crash report holds the crash's logs,
          diagnostics and app performance data; the usage count says which screens were opened.
          Firebase gives the app an app-instance identifier of its own, and that travels with both.
          Google holds all of it, as Firebase's processor, for the Fugaro project, under{' '}
          <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's Firebase
          privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Never sent: the contents of any message, anything from your installation or its mailbox,
          your credentials and your keys. The reports are read to find and fix crashes and to see
          which screens are used, and for nothing else. The app carries no advertising identifier.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app versions that carry these reports send them. There is no switch inside the app to
          turn them off: uninstalling the app stops them, and not installing it avoids them. The
          command line tool and the console are unaffected: they carry none of these reports.
          Earlier builds of the app send nothing at all.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card privacy-card">
        <h3>Deleting your data</h3>
        <p>
          Everything else the software holds lives in your own accounts, your own cloud project and
          your own devices, and removing the installation removes it. The crash reports and
          anonymous usage above are the exception, and deleting the app does not remove them:
          Google holds them for the Fugaro project, under the policy linked above. Uninstalling the
          app stops the sending. It does not take a report already sent out of Google's hands, and
          the issue tracker is where to ask about one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card privacy-card">
        <h3>Contact</h3>
        <p>
          Questions and bug reports go to the repository's{' '}
          <a href={`${repo}/issues`} rel="noopener">issue tracker</a>. Report a vulnerability
          through <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md#reporting-a-vulnerability`} rel="noopener">SECURITY.md</a>'s
          private route, never in a public issue.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Scoped, so nothing here can reach another page, and token-only: the palette is W1.2's and
     this file carries no colour literal (website/test/a11y.mjs refuses one outside :root).

     The look itself is `src/styles/base.css`'s: the display scale, the gradient, the card and
     the hero's glow are all shapes that file already draws, and what is here is the layout
     this page gives them — the page's own column, the spacing between its parts, and the one
     place a shape needed a nudge (a card's heading sits at the card's own edge). Nothing in
     this block is a new visual idea, which is why no shared file moved for this page. */
  .privacy {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-xl) var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The hero: `base.css`'s glow is absolutely positioned over its parent and masked to fade
     before the parent's end, so the parent is the positioned, clipped box — the top of the
     page, down to the lead. The headline and the lead are lifted above the layer. */
  .privacy-hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) 0;
  }

  .privacy-title,
  .privacy-lead {
    position: relative;
  }

  .privacy-lead {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The two parts, and the cards under them. Both part headings are children of this box, so
     the gate reads them as the page's two `h2`s, in order, at one level (`privacy.mjs`). */
  .privacy-body {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .privacy-part {
    margin: var(--space-3xl) 0 var(--space-md);
  }

  .privacy-body > .privacy-part:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .privacy-card + .privacy-card {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* A card's heading, set the way the site's other cards set theirs (`HomeWhatYouGet.astro`):
     the heading step of the type scale at the display weight, so a card reads as a card
     wherever it stands. */
  .privacy-card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }

  .privacy p,
  .privacy li {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .privacy li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .privacy ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
  }

  .privacy code {
    background: var(--panel);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0 var(--space-3xs);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
